WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.881
AI bots can write code and run tasks. But how should they get permission
to spend money?

2
00:00:05.881 --> 00:00:15.267
MoneySwitch, created by hecare, is a programmable Web3 cloud wallet for
AI bots. Your cloud. Your wallet. Your rules.

3
00:00:15.267 --> 00:00:25.490
The problem grows across laptops, servers, and cloud agents. Giving every
bot its own wallet means more private keys, balances, and approvals to
manage.

4
00:00:25.490 --> 00:00:33.657
One funder runs a wallet service on their own server. Agents receive
separate spending keys. The wallet's private key stays with the service.

5
00:00:33.657 --> 00:00:46.243
Each key has enforceable rules: total, daily, and per-payment limits,
approved hosts, expiry, and revocation. The model requests a payment; the
service enforces the policy.

6
00:00:46.243 --> 00:00:55.324
Payments at or above an approval threshold wait for a person. Hard limits
still apply. Bills show which key paid, how much, and where.

7
00:00:55.324 --> 00:01:06.919
Today, MoneySwitch uses USDC and the x402 payment flow, supporting Monad
and Base. Agents can purchase compatible paid APIs without managing
blockchain keys.

8
00:01:06.919 --> 00:01:18.133
In one recorded Qwen research run, five Nansen purchases settled on Monad
mainnet, totaling nine cents in USDC. The published write-up links to the
transactions.

9
00:01:18.133 --> 00:01:26.300
This is an early, self-hosted product. The server remains a hot-wallet
trust boundary, so keep it isolated from agent system permissions.

10
00:01:26.300 --> 00:01:37.771
Try two agents with separate budgets on testnet. Share deployment and
approval feedback, improve the docs, or open a pull request. Help build
MoneySwitch.
